Parallel streams.

Here is the promise this module has been building toward. Because a pipeline describes what to compute, and not how to loop, the same code can run across every core of your machine with a one-word change. Parallel-stream — or dot-parallel on an existing stream — splits the work across threads. But that power comes with sharp edges, and knowing when not to use it matters more than knowing how.

Start with the one-word switch. Sequential and parallel share the identical pipeline; only the source changes. Orders dot stream, filter to the big ones, count — that's sequential, one thread. Orders dot parallel-stream, filter to the big ones, count — that's parallel, many threads. Under the hood, the framework splits the source into chunks, runs the pipeline on each chunk on a different thread, drawn from a shared fork-join common pool, and then combines the partial results. You wrote a description; the runtime chose the execution.

Now, why does your reducer's contract suddenly matter? Everything in section six about associativity and neutral identities was laying the groundwork for exactly this moment. In parallel, elements are combined out of order, and in separate groups, and then merged. That is only correct if three things hold. The combining operator must be associative, so that regrouping doesn't change the answer. The identity must be truly neutral. And each element must be processed independently — the function for one element must not depend on, or mutate, shared state touched by another.

Break that independence, and you get the classic bug. A for-each that adds to a shared array-list, or a lambda that mutates a field, races across threads and produces corrupt or nondeterministic results — no exception, just wrong answers, some of the time. And the fix is never a lock. It's to stay functional: use collect or reduce with a proper collector, which the framework merges safely, and never mutate shared state from inside a pipeline.

Let's be honest about cost, too. Parallelism is not free. Splitting the source, dispatching to threads, and merging results all have overhead. It only wins when the work is big enough to swamp that overhead. So here's a mental checklist. First, enough data — thousands of elements, not dozens; on a small stream, the coordination costs more than the loop it replaces. Second, cheap to split — an array-list or an array splits in constant time by index, while a linked-list, or most iterate sources, split badly and gain little. Third, expensive per element, or a great many elements, so the real arithmetic dominates the coordination. And fourth, no ordering tax — order-sensitive operations like limit and find-first fight parallelism, whereas find-any and unordered work parallelize cleanly.

And here's a hard rule: never parallelize a pipeline whose lambdas touch shared mutable state, or do blocking input-output. The common pool is shared across the whole process, so a blocked parallel stream can starve every other one.

So what's the honest default? Reach for parallel-stream only when you have measured a real, CPU-bound bottleneck over a large dataset — and then measure again, to confirm it actually helped. For the overwhelming majority of pipelines, sequential is the right choice: simpler, deterministic, and often faster once you count the overhead. Parallel streams are a precision tool, not a default upgrade to dot-stream. The value of this module is that when you do need them, the code barely changes.
